# 🐳 Docker Implementation

## 4. Created the Backend Dockerfile

The first Docker configuration was created for the JerseyHub backend.

### File

```text
backend/Dockerfile
```

### Dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM node:24-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./

RUN npm ci --omit=dev

COPY . .

ENV NODE_ENV=production

EXPOSE 2987

CMD ["npm", "start"]
```

---

## 5. Understanding the Backend Dockerfile

### Base Image

```dockerfile
FROM node:24-alpine
```

Uses the lightweight Alpine-based Node.js 24 image as the base environment.

---

### Working Directory

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /app
```

Creates and sets `/app` as the working directory inside the container.

Subsequent Docker commands operate from this directory.

---

### Copy Dependency Files

```dockerfile
COPY package*.json ./
```

Copies the Node.js dependency files before copying the complete application.

This allows Docker's layer caching to avoid reinstalling dependencies unnecessarily when only application source code changes.

---

### Install Production Dependencies

```dockerfile
RUN npm ci --omit=dev
```

Installs dependencies using the lock file.

`npm ci` provides reproducible dependency installation, while:

```text
--omit=dev
```

prevents development-only dependencies from being installed in the production image.

---

### Copy Application Code

```dockerfile
COPY . .
```

Copies the backend application source code into the container.

---

### Production Environment

```dockerfile
ENV NODE_ENV=production
```

Configures Node.js to run in production mode.

---

### Application Port

```dockerfile
EXPOSE 2987
```

Documents that the backend application expects to listen on port:

```text
2987
```

---

### Start the Backend

```dockerfile
CMD ["npm", "start"]
```

Starts the Express backend when the container launches.

---

# 🧹 Docker Build Context

## 6. Created `.dockerignore`

A `.dockerignore` file was created for the backend.

### File

```text
backend/.dockerignore
```

### Configuration

```dockerignore
node_modules
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
coverage
.DS_Store
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
```

The purpose of `.dockerignore` is to prevent unnecessary or sensitive files from being sent to Docker during the image build process.

---

## Why These Files Are Ignored

### `node_modules`

```text
node_modules
```

Prevents locally installed dependencies from being copied into the Linux container.

Dependencies should instead be installed inside the image using:

```bash
npm ci
```

---

### Environment Files

```text
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
```

Prevents real environment files and credentials from entering Docker image layers.

An example environment file may still be kept for documentation.

---

### Logs

```text
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
```

Prevents application and npm logs from increasing the Docker build context and image size.

---

### Coverage

```text
coverage
```

Prevents generated testing coverage files from being included in the production image.

---

### System Files

```text
.DS_Store
```

Prevents unnecessary operating-system metadata files from entering the image.

---

### Docker Configuration Files

```text
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
```

Prevents Docker configuration files themselves from being copied into the application filesystem inside the final image.

